## Code rules

- Prefer pure CSS on the selector `.texture`: layered and repeating gradients, and SVG noise as a `data:image/svg+xml` URI using feTurbulence. A CSS answer is the rule set alone, with no `<style>` tag. Set `kind` to `css`.
- Fall back to a small seeded canvas script only when CSS cannot draw the texture, such as brush strokes, grass blades or felt fibres. A canvas answer is `<canvas class="texture" width="640" height="400"></canvas>` followed by a `<script>` that draws it with its own seeded random, so it looks the same on every load. Set `kind` to `canvas`. Include any helper the drawing needs; nothing from the reference exists on the page.
- No external images, fonts or scripts: no http or https URLs other than the SVG namespace, and no `url()` other than a `data:` URI.
- Under {{ max_bytes }} bytes of code. Stay well under.
- Text in the light colour will sit on this texture. In `contrast`, state how it stays at WCAG AA (4.5 to 1 for body text): either it already does because the texture stays close to the ground's lightness, or say which scrim you added or how you lowered the texture's contrast.
- In `warning`: when the texture drawn is dithering, ASCII or a gradient (grainy, thermal or plain) and nothing in the subject calls for it, write one sentence saying it reads as an AI default and naming the texture from the subject's world to use instead. Otherwise leave it empty.
- Put the drawn texture's catalog name in `texture`, exactly as listed above.
